# Line endings are LF in the working tree, on every platform.
#
# Without this, a Windows checkout with `core.autocrlf=true` (Git for Windows' default) writes CRLF
# into the working tree, and `pnpm format:check` fails on *every* file — Prettier's `endOfLine`
# defaults to `lf` and it checks the working tree, not the index. The gate was unpassable on Windows
# for that reason alone, on 82 files nobody had misformatted.
#
# `eol=lf` overrides `core.autocrlf`, so this fixes the checkout rather than asking each developer to
# change a global Git setting. It changes no committed content: `text=auto` has always normalised
# these files to LF on the way in, which is why adding this produced no diff.
#
# The alternative considered and rejected was `endOfLine: "auto"` in .prettierrc. It would silence
# the gate in one line, but it silences it by accepting whichever ending each file happens to have —
# so a genuinely mixed file would pass. This enforces the thing the gate is meant to check.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary files, listed explicitly rather than left to detection: a false positive on any of these
# would corrupt them by rewriting bytes that are not line endings.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.pdf binary
